.TH MEM 4 "January 2026" "Substrate Kernel" "Device Drivers Manual"
.SH NAME
mem, kmem \- physical and kernel virtual memory
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /dev/mem
.br
.B /dev/kmem
.SH DESCRIPTION
.SS /dev/mem
The
.B mem
device provides access to the computer's physical memory.
Byte addresses in
.B /dev/mem
are interpreted as physical memory addresses.
.PP
This device is typically used for:
.IP \(bu 2
Accessing memory-mapped I/O regions
.IP \(bu 2
Reading BIOS data areas
.IP \(bu 2
Hardware diagnostics
.SS /dev/kmem
The
.B kmem
device provides access to the kernel's virtual address space.
Byte addresses correspond to kernel virtual addresses.
.PP
This device allows inspection of kernel data structures and is primarily
used by system utilities such as
.BR ps (1)
and
.BR top (1)
on traditional Unix systems.
.SH SECURITY
Access to these devices requires root privileges.
Reading or writing arbitrary memory can crash the system or compromise security.
.PP
Modern security practices recommend disabling these devices or restricting
access via mandatory access controls.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /dev/mem
Physical memory (character device, major 1, minor 1).
.TP
.B /dev/kmem
Kernel virtual memory (character device, major 1, minor 2).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR intro (4)
